### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

core

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

An element bound with `[animate.leave]` whose value resolves to an empty class list (`''`, `[]`) is never released from the module-level `leavingNodes` map after it is removed, so every such removal retains the detached element and its declaration view for as long as the template's `TNode` lives.

On removal, `node_manipulation` calls `trackLeavingNodes(tNode, el, lView)` because a leave animation is registered for the node. `runLeaveAnimations` then takes the branch for an empty class list and only calls `resolve()`:

```js
const activeClasses = getClassListFromValue(value);
if (activeClasses && activeClasses.length > 0) {
  animateLeaveClassRunner(nativeElement, tNode, lView, activeClasses, renderer, ngZone);
} else {
  resolve();
}
```

`clearLeavingNodes(tNode, el)` is reached only from `animateLeaveClassRunner` and from `leaveAnimationFunctionCleanup` (the function form), never from that branch. The `{ el, declarationView }` entry stays in `leavingNodes` (a `WeakMap` keyed by the `TNode`, which lives as long as the component's template), and the array grows by one entry per removal.

A conditional binding is the natural way to make an exit animation optional, e.g. `[animate.leave]="exitClass()"` with `exitClass()` returning `''` when no animation is configured, so any `@for` rendering items under such a binding leaks one detached subtree per removed item.

### Reproduction

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <button (click)="add()">add</button>
    <button (click)="items.set([])">clear</button>
    @for (item of items(); track item) {
      <div [animate.leave]="leave()">{{ item }}</div>
    }
  `,
})
export class App {
  readonly items = signal<number[]>([]);
  readonly leave = signal('');
  #next = 0;
  add(): void {
    this.items.update((items) => [...items, this.#next++]);
  }
}
```

Click "add" then "clear" repeatedly, force a GC (DevTools → Memory → collect garbage) and read the DOM node count (`Memory.getDOMCounters` over CDP, or the Performance monitor). The count grows with every cycle, and a heap snapshot shows the retaining path:

```text
leavingNodes (WeakMap) → Array → [n] { el, declarationView } → .el → <div> → …
```

With `leave` set to a non-empty class (even one with no CSS animation, which resolves through the `!longest` branch), the count stays flat.

Measured on Chromium with forced GC before each reading, in a library whose rows carry a small subtree: +13 and +16 detached DOM nodes per removed item in a production build, steady across 20, 20 and 40 cycles, and +26 per item in a development build. Event listeners do not grow.

### Expected behavior

Removing an element whose `animate.leave` resolves to no classes releases it like a removal with no binding at all: the empty-class branch of `runLeaveAnimations` should call `clearLeavingNodes(tNode, nativeElement)` (and clear the node's resolvers) before resolving.

### Please provide the environment you discovered this bug in

```
Angular: 22.2.4
Browser: Chromium
```

### Anything else?

Workaround in a library template: render the element without the `[animate.leave]` binding when the class is empty (two branches of an `@if`), or use the function form `(animate.leave)` and always call `event.animationComplete()`.

Alternative to #72101: instead of moving `ɵsetClassMetadata` / `ɵsetClassMetadataAsync` out of `static {}` in `ngp` whenever a class has surviving member or method-parameter decorators, this PR updates `ngtsc` to emit `ɵsetClassMetadata` / `ɵsetClassMetadataAsync` inside a `static { ... }` block on the class whenever the class is not wrapped by `Cls = __decorate(..., Cls)` (i.e. when there are no surviving non-Angular class or constructor-parameter decorators).

When surviving non-Angular class or constructor-parameter decorators are present, both `ngtsc` and `ngp` emit `ɵsetClassMetadata` as a trailing IIFE after `__decorate` (`ng-analyze` adds `has_non_angular_ctor_param_decorators` so `ngp` matches `ngtsc` for constructor-parameter decorators). Because `ngtsc` and `ngp` now agree on `static {}` placement, this also removes the `ctx.complianceMode` special case for `ɵsetClassMetadata` in `ngp`.

Split into 3 commits for reviewability:
1. **`ngtsc` & `ngp` implementation + unit specs** (`transform.ts`, `processor.ts`, `ng-analyze`, `ngtsc_spec.ts`)
2. **`@angular/build` `oxc-transform` static-block metadata elision** (temporary `pnpm` patch corresponding to upstream PR angular/angular-cli#34770 so full-mode `@angular/core` classes with `static {}` metadata continue to be pure-wrapped and tree-shaken)
3. **Compliance `.js` golden updates** (`expect_emit.ts` + 591 compliance goldens)

### Minimal failure example

```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';

function Log(): MethodDecorator {
  return (_t, _k, d) => d;
}

@Component({selector: 'my-cmp', template: ''})
export class MyCmp {
  @Log()
  greet() {}
}
```

**Before (`main` `ngtsc`):**
```js
export class MyCmp {
  greet() {}
  static ɵfac = function MyCmp_Factory(__ngFactoryType__) { return new (__ngFactoryType__ || MyCmp)(); };
  static ɵcmp = /*@__PURE__*/ i0.ɵɵdefineComponent({ type: MyCmp, selectors: [["my-cmp"]], decls: 0, vars: 0, template: function MyCmp_Template(rf, ctx) {} });
}
__decorate([
  Log()
], MyCmp.prototype, "greet", null);
(() => {
  (typeof ngDevMode === "undefined" || ngDevMode) &&
    i0.ɵsetClassMetadata(MyCmp, [{ type: Component, args: [{ selector: 'my-cmp', template: '' }] }], null, null);
})();
// On pre-ES2022 targets (e.g. ES2020), `ngp` downleveled `static {}` into an IIFE *before*
// `__decorate([Log()], MyCmp.prototype, ...)`, whereas `ngtsc` emitted its IIFE *after* `__decorate`.
```

**After (`ngtsc` & `ngp`):**
```js
export class MyCmp {
  greet() {}
  static ɵfac = function MyCmp_Factory(__ngFactoryType__) { return new (__ngFactoryType__ || MyCmp)(); };
  static ɵcmp = /*@__PURE__*/ i0.ɵɵdefineComponent({ type: MyCmp, selectors: [["my-cmp"]], decls: 0, vars: 0, template: function MyCmp_Template(rf, ctx) {} });
  static {
    (typeof ngDevMode === "undefined" || ngDevMode) &&
      i0.ɵsetClassMetadata(MyCmp, [{ type: Component, args: [{ selector: 'my-cmp', template: '' }] }], null, null);
  }
}
__decorate([
  Log()
], MyCmp.prototype, "greet", null);
// On pre-ES2022 targets, TypeScript downlevels `static {}` into an IIFE immediately after `MyCmp.ɵcmp`
// and *before* `MyCmp.prototype` `__decorate` in both `ngtsc` and `ngp`. Keeping `ɵsetClassMetadata`
// inside `static {}` also keeps any instance `#private` field references in decorator metadata
// lexically inside the class body instead of producing a JS `SyntaxError`.
```

### Reservations (vs. #72101)

1. **Requires `@angular/build` (`oxc-transform`) update first (angular/angular-cli#34770)**: Commit 2 temporarily patches `@angular/build` via `patchedDependencies` so `oxc-transform` removes `ngDevMode`/`ngJitMode`-guarded `(ɵ)setClassMetadata*` `StaticBlock`s and wraps pure static Angular classes in `@angular/core/fesm2022/core.mjs` (`//packages/core/test/bundling/*` and `integration/standalone-bootstrap`); before landing, angular/angular-cli#34770 should release and roll in normally (which `ngp` also needs before `packages/core` switches to `ngp`).
2. **Compliance golden churn & partial-linker divergence**: Updating `ngtsc` modifies 591 compliance `.js` goldens (and shifts ES5 downleveled `ɵsetClassMetadata` inside the ES5 class closure right before `return Cls;`). Partial compilation (`GOLDEN_PARTIAL.js`) keeps `ɵɵngDeclareClassMetadata` as a top-level statement after the class to preserve the v12.0.0 partial-declaration format, so linked output still emits a trailing IIFE (normalized in `expect_emit.ts` via `relocateStaticClassMetadataForLinked`).
3. **Execution order relative to member / static `__decorate`**: In dev / `TestBed` (`ngDevMode = true`), `ɵsetClassMetadata` inside `static {}` now executes *before* `tslib.__decorate` runs on prototype or static members. If an Angular decorator expression re-emitted in `ɵsetClassMetadata` eagerly calls a `@Decorated() static` method on the same class, or if a custom member decorator writes directly to `target.constructor.propDecorators`, `ɵsetClassMetadata` runs before that decorator rather than after it.
4. **Classes wrapped by `__decorate` still emit outside `static {}`**: While this eliminates the ~600-line `OutsideMetadataScope` `@ts-ignore` visibility-tracking machinery needed by #72101 for member and method-parameter decorators, classes with surviving custom class or constructor-parameter decorators still emit `ɵsetClassMetadata` outside the class after `Cls = __decorate([...], Cls)`.

Please note that this fixes a **real** production issue; it wasn't found speculatively through AI code analysis.

<img width="694" height="111" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a4f28706-97a2-41a2-bf79-b22cc9113091" />

Consider the following example:

```ts
@Component({
  template: '@for (item of items; track item) {{{ item }}}',
})
class App {
  items = {0: 'a', 1: 'b'};
}
```

Prior to this commit, `reconcile()` treated every non-nullish value that is not an array as an iterable and called `Symbol.iterator` on it without a check. A plain object therefore failed with a raw `TypeError`:

```
TypeError: t[Symbol.iterator] is not a function
```

That error carries no code, does not mention `@for`, and does not say which template produced it. In a production build the stack is minified, so error reporting shows nothing actionable. The same value through `*ngFor` reports NG02200, which names the problem and suggests the keyvalue pipe, so `@for` was harder to debug than the API it replaces.

With this commit the same value throws NG0957. Only the message text is behind `ngDevMode`; the check itself is not, because production is where the raw error is unreadable today. The array fast path is untouched, and `null` and `undefined` keep rendering nothing.

The code is negative, which is how this package marks codes that have a guide on angular.dev, so the fix ships with one.

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

core

### Is this a regression?

No

It reproduces on 20.3.35, 21.2.26 and 22.3.0 (the current `latest`), in development and production builds.

### Description

With hydration enabled, content projected through an `<ng-content>` slot is lost when all of these hold:

1. A component template (`WrapperComponent`) places an `<ng-content />` inside an element, for example `<div><ng-content /></div>`, in the content of another component (`<app-container>`).
2. The other component (`ContainerComponent`) does not render its own `<ng-content>` on the server, for example because the slot sits inside an `@if` that is `false`.
3. The condition becomes `true` after hydration.

The server render succeeds, hydration reports no error, and the server DOM is reused. But once the container opens, it shows an empty `<div>`: the projected text is missing. It stays missing after closing and opening the container again. Nothing is logged in the console.

Without `provideClientHydration()`, or with the condition already `true` on the server, the text is there.

Results in headless Chromium (Chrome for Testing 156). The development and production builds give the same results:

| Template inside `<app-container [open]="open()">` | `open` on the server | `provideClientHydration()` | `<app-container>` after opening |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `<div><ng-content /></div>` | `false` | yes | `<div></div>` |
| `<div><ng-content /></div>` | `false` | no | `<div>projected text</div>` |
| `<div><ng-content /></div>` | `true` | yes | `<div>projected text</div>` |

Boundaries, with hydration and `open` set to `false` on the server. `<app-box>` is a component whose template is only `<ng-content />`.

| Template inside `<app-container [open]="open()">` | `<app-container>` after opening |
| --- | --- |
| `<div><span><ng-content /></span></div>` | `<div><span></span></div>` |
| `<div><ng-container><ng-content /></ng-container></div>` | `<div></div>` |
| `<div><b>before</b><ng-content /></div>` | `<div><b>before</b></div>` |
| `<div><ng-content /></div>`, with `<b>projected element</b>` projected into `<app-wrapper>` | `<div></div>` |
| `<div><ng-content select="b" /><ng-content /></div>`, with `<b>b</b> text` projected | `<div></div>` |
| `<app-box><div><ng-content /></div></app-box>` | `<app-box><div></div></app-box>` |
| `<ng-content />` | `projected text` (correct) |
| `<ng-container><ng-content /></ng-container>` | `projected text` (correct) |
| `@if (true) { <div><ng-content /></div> }` | `<div>projected text</div>` (correct) |
| `<div><app-box><ng-content /></app-box></div>` | `<div><app-box>projected text</app-box></div>` (correct) |

In every row, the page hydrates with no NG05xx error and reuses the server-rendered `<button>`, `<app-wrapper>` and `<app-container>`.

