### Anything else?

#### Cause

On the server, `<app-container>` does not render its slot, so the `<div>` and the projected text never reach the document. `serializeLView` records them as disconnected: in the `__nghData__` above, `"d":[1]` is the `<div>` in `WrapperComponent` and `"d":[3]` is the text in `App`.

On the client:

1. The text node in `App` is created, because it is disconnected. It is not inserted anywhere yet: its parent is the `<app-wrapper>` host, and the content of a component is inserted by that component's `ɵɵprojection`.
2. In `WrapperComponent`, `ɵɵelementStart` for the `<div>` calls `canHydrateNode`, which returns `false` for a disconnected node, so a new `<div>` is created.
3. `ɵɵprojection` for the `<ng-content />` inside that `<div>` skips `applyProjection`, because the view has hydration info.
4. When `open` becomes `true`, the `@if` view of `ContainerComponent` is created without hydration info. Its `ɵɵprojection` moves the `<div>` into `<app-container>`, and the `<div>` is still empty.

Step 3 is in [`packages/core/src/render3/instructions/projection.ts#L194-L204`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/73916540c1688c0463617ef1469bf63adb0a628f/packages/core/src/render3/instructions/projection.ts#L194-L204) (`main` at `7391654`):

```ts
  const hydrationInfo = lView[HYDRATION];
  const isNodeCreationMode = !hydrationInfo || isInSkipHydrationBlock();
  const componentHostNode = lView[DECLARATION_COMPONENT_VIEW][T_HOST] as TElementNode;
  const isEmpty = componentHostNode.projection![tProjectionNode.projection] === null;

  if (isEmpty && fallbackIndex !== null) {
    insertFallbackContent(lView, tView, fallbackIndex);
  } else if (isNodeCreationMode && !isDetachedByI18n(tProjectionNode)) {
    // re-distribution of projectable nodes is stored on a component's view level
    applyProjection(tView, lView, tProjectionNode);
  }
```

Skipping `applyProjection` while hydrating is right when the slot's parent was hydrated, because the projected nodes are already in it. Here the parent was created on the client, so the projected nodes were created on the client too, and nothing inserts them.

The rows of the boundaries table that work match this reading:

- With `<ng-content />` directly in `<app-container>`, or inside an `<ng-container>`, step 4 inserts the text, because `applyProjectionRecursive` walks into nested `<ng-content>` slots and into the children of `<ng-container>`. It does not walk into the children of an element.
- With `<div><app-box><ng-content /></app-box></div>`, the host of `<app-box>` is created on the client, so its view has no hydration info and its own `ɵɵprojection` inserts the text.
- With `@if (true) { <div><ng-content /></div> }`, the `@if` view is created without hydration info, so its `ɵɵprojection` runs in creation mode.

#### Proposed fix

Treat the slot as being in creation mode when its parent was not hydrated. `canHydrateNode` ([`packages/core/src/hydration/utils.ts#L523-L532`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/73916540c1688c0463617ef1469bf63adb0a628f/packages/core/src/hydration/utils.ts#L523-L532)) is the check that the element, `<ng-container>`, text and template instructions already use, through their `locateOrCreate…` functions, to decide whether to create a node. Applied to the slot's parent, it tells whether that parent was created on the client.

```diff
--- a/packages/core/src/render3/instructions/projection.ts
+++ b/packages/core/src/render3/instructions/projection.ts
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
  * Use of this source code is governed by an MIT-style license that can be
  * found in the LICENSE file at https://angular.dev/license
  */
+import {canHydrateNode} from '../../hydration/utils';
 import {findMatchingDehydratedView} from '../../hydration/views';
 import {isDetachedByI18n} from '../../i18n/utils';
 import {newArray} from '../../util/array_utils';
@@ -192,7 +193,15 @@
   setCurrentTNodeAsNotParent();

   const hydrationInfo = lView[HYDRATION];
-  const isNodeCreationMode = !hydrationInfo || isInSkipHydrationBlock();
+  const parentTNode = tProjectionNode.parent;
+  // While hydrating, the projected nodes are already in place in the DOM, so they are not
+  // inserted again. The exception is a slot whose parent was created on the client: it was
+  // disconnected from the DOM on the server (for example, inside content that was not
+  // projected), so the projected nodes were created on the client as well and must be inserted.
+  const isNodeCreationMode =
+    !hydrationInfo ||
+    isInSkipHydrationBlock() ||
+    (parentTNode !== null && !canHydrateNode(lView, parentTNode));
   const componentHostNode = lView[DECLARATION_COMPONENT_VIEW][T_HOST] as TElementNode;
   const isEmpty = componentHostNode.projection![tProjectionNode.projection] === null;
```

`text.ts`, `element.ts`, `element_container.ts` and `template.ts` in the same folder already import `canHydrateNode` from `../../hydration/utils`.

I applied the same condition to the compiled `@angular/core` 22.3.0 of the reproduction (`fesm2022/_debug_node-chunk.mjs`, in `ɵɵprojection`) and ran every row above, in development and production builds:

- Every row that lost the content now shows it: `<div>projected text</div>`, `<div><span>projected text</span></div>`, `<div><b>before</b>projected text</div>`, `<div><b>projected element</b></div>`, `<div><b>b</b> text</div>`, `<app-box><div>projected text</div></app-box>`. It hides on the second click and shows again on the third.
- The rows that already worked give the same content as without the change, including `open` set to `true` on the server, where the server-rendered `<div>` is reused.
- The server output does not change: the prerendered HTML and `__nghData__` are identical with and without the change. The change only affects the client, since `withDomHydration` does not enable the hydration runtime when `ngServerMode` is set, so `lView[HYDRATION]` is `null` on the server.
- Hydration does not change: the server-rendered `<button>`, `<app-wrapper>` and `<app-container>` are reused, the development build logs the same `Angular hydrated N component(s) and M node(s)` counts as without the change, and there is no NG05xx error or any other console output.

