### Description

With hydration enabled, server rendering fails with `NG0502: During serialization, Angular was unable to find an element in the DOM` when all of these hold:

1. A component template (`WrapperComponent`) places `<ng-content />` inside the content of another component (`<app-container>`).
2. In that content, a node follows the `<ng-content />` as a sibling: a control-flow block, an element, a text node, an `<ng-container>`, or a `@for`.
3. The other component (`ContainerComponent`) does not render its own `<ng-content>` on the server, for example because the slot sits inside an `@if` that is false.

Content that is not projected is a supported case. Hydration marks such nodes as disconnected (`appendDisconnectedNodeIndex`), and the existing test "should support cases when some element nodes are not projected (@if)" covers it. The failure only happens when a sibling follows the `<ng-content />`.

The same failure happens with no `<ng-content>` at all when a `@let` declaration is followed by a sibling in content that is not projected. `@let` and `<ng-content>` share the same branch of `serializeLView`.

Boundaries (`ng build`, which prerenders `/`):

| Template inside `<app-container [open]="open()">` | `open` on the server | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `<ng-content />@if (true) { <p>after</p> }` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content />@if (true) { <p>after</p> }` | `true` | OK |
| `<ng-content />` | `false` | OK |
| `<ng-content /><p>after</p>` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content />after` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content /><ng-container>after</ng-container>` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content />@for (i of [1]; track i) { <p>{{ i }}</p> }` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<div><ng-content />@if (true) { <p>after</p> }</div>` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<p>before</p><ng-content />` | `false` | OK |
| `<ng-content select="b" /><ng-content />` | `false` | OK |
| `@let x = 1;@if (true) { <p>{{ x }}</p> }` (no `<ng-content>`) | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content />@let x = 1;` | `false` | OK |

The bug does not depend on what is projected into `<app-wrapper>`: it also fails when nothing is projected.

With `RenderMode.Server` instead of prerendering (and `localhost` in `security.allowedHosts`), the request answers HTTP 200 with the body `Internal server error.` and nothing is logged, because the error happens while the response streams.

