#### Cause

In `serializeLView`, the branch for `<ng-content>` slots and `@let` declarations stores a full path for the node that follows them, because that node cannot be located from a sibling anchor. It does not check whether that node is disconnected:

[`packages/core/src/hydration/annotate.ts#L707-L722`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/73916540c1688c0463617ef1469bf63adb0a628f/packages/core/src/hydration/annotate.ts#L707-L722) (`main` at `7391654`):

```ts
      } else if (tNode.type & (TNodeType.Projection | TNodeType.LetDeclaration)) {
        // ...
        let nextTNode = tNode.next;
        // Skip over all `<ng-content>` slots and `@let` declarations in a row.
        while (
          nextTNode !== null &&
          nextTNode.type & (TNodeType.Projection | TNodeType.LetDeclaration)
        ) {
          nextTNode = nextTNode.next;
        }
        if (nextTNode && !isInSkipHydrationBlock(nextTNode)) {
          // Handle a tNode after the `<ng-content>` slot.
          appendSerializedNodePath(ngh, nextTNode, lView, i18nChildren);
        }
```

When the content is not projected, the next node (here the `@if` anchor comment) never reaches the document. `calcPathForNode` cannot find it from its parent or from `<body>`, and it throws `nodeNotFoundError`.

The `<ng-content>` / `@let` slot itself never counts as disconnected, because `isDisconnectedNode` excludes `Projection` and `LetDeclaration` nodes, so it always reaches this branch. The sibling's own iteration does record it as disconnected at [line 613](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/73916540c1688c0463617ef1469bf63adb0a628f/packages/core/src/hydration/annotate.ts#L613), but this branch computes a path for it anyway. The projection-head branch at [lines 632-640](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/73916540c1688c0463617ef1469bf63adb0a628f/packages/core/src/hydration/annotate.ts#L632-L640) already guards the same situation with `isDisconnectedNode`.

#### Proposed fix

```diff
-        if (nextTNode && !isInSkipHydrationBlock(nextTNode)) {
+        if (nextTNode && !isInSkipHydrationBlock(nextTNode) && !isDisconnectedNode(nextTNode, lView)) {
```

I applied this change to the compiled `@angular/core` (`fesm2022/core.mjs`) of the reproduction on 22.2.1, 22.3.0-rc.0 and 22.3.0:

- Every row of the boundaries table builds and prerenders.
- The disconnected sibling is still serialized as disconnected by its own iteration. For the reproduction, `__nghData__` is `[{"t":{"0":"t0"},"c":{"0":[]}},{"t":{"2":"t1"},"d":[2]},{"d":[3]}]`.
- In Chromium (headless, dev and prod builds), the page hydrates. The dev build logs `Angular hydrated 3 component(s) and 9 node(s), 0 component(s) were skipped`, with no NG05xx errors in the console. The server-rendered `<button>`, `<app-wrapper>` and `<app-container>` elements are reused.
- After hydration, toggling `open` renders `projected text<p>after</p>` inside `<app-container>`. It hides on the second click and renders again on the third.

An equivalent alternative is to mirror the projection-head branch (`isDisconnectedNode` → `appendDisconnectedNodeIndex`, else `appendSerializedNodePath`). It produced the same `d` indices and the same browser result. Unlike the one-line guard, it would also record the sibling as disconnected when it is not under a component host, which the line 613 check does not cover (I did not test that case).

