### Description

`CdkTreeNode` and `MatTreeNode` both bind `[attr.aria-posinset]` to `_getPositionInSet()`, which asks the tree, and [`CdkTree._getPositionInSet()`](https://github.com/angular/components/blob/v22.3.0/src/cdk/tree/tree.ts#L858-L862) searches the node's set for it:

```ts
_getPositionInSet(dataNode: T) {
  const set = this._getAriaSet(dataNode);
  const key = this._getExpansionKey(dataNode);
  return set.findIndex(node => this._getExpansionKey(node) === key) + 1;
}
```

So a check of the k-th child of a parent compares k nodes, in each of the two bindings of a `mat-tree-node`, and a check of all n children of an expanded parent makes n(n + 1) comparisons (half as many for a `cdk-tree-node`, which has one binding).

Angular checks every rendered node at each click, focus and keydown in the tree. `CdkTree` listens to `keydown`, and `CdkTreeNode` to `click` and `focus`. A listener marks its view and every view above it for refresh, the tree's among them, and refreshing the tree's view refreshes the view of every node, as they are embedded views in it. A render checks them too: `renderNodeChanges()` calls `detectChanges()` when it renders the tree's own nodes. So each click or key takes time in the square of the siblings: the sum of n(n + 1) over the sets of rendered siblings. In the reproduction below, with 40,000 children under one expanded parent, a click took 2.0 s and ArrowDown 1.9 s, and twice as many children took about four times as long. We met it in a tree of a folder's files, where a folder can hold tens of thousands.

The search came with `aria-posinset` in 18.2.0 (#29062), and it is the same in 22.3.0 and on `main`.

**A suggested fix**: store each node's index in its set when the sets are built, and look it up. Against `src/cdk/tree/tree.ts` of 22.3.0 and `main`:

